ns skirt cities as moving platforms ferry passengers to and from the city centre.", "reference": "2013 June 1, “Ideas coming down the track”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8838, page 13 (Technology Quarterly):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To carry or transport over a contracted body of water, as a river or strait, in a boat or other floating conveyance plying between opposite shores.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To pass over water in a boat or by ferry.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "They ferry over this Lethean sound / Both to and fro.", "reference": "1667, John Milton, “Book II”, in Paradise Lost. […], London: […] [Samuel Simmons], and are to be sold by Peter Parker […]; [a]nd by Robert Boulter […]; [a]nd Matthias Walker, […], →OCLC; republished as Paradise Lost in Ten Books: […], London: Basil Montagu Pickering […], 1873, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfɛɹi/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfeɹi/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfɛɾe/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfɛɾɪ/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfɛɾi/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}], "forms": [{"word": "ferries", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(nautical) A boat or ship used to transport people, smaller vehicles and goods from one port to another, usually on a regular schedule.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Near-synonym: ferryboat", "Islanders normally have to queue for half an hour to get on the ferry."], "quotes": [{"text": 